'''Team Falcons''' is a [[:Category:Saudi Arabia|Saudi Arabian]] esports organization.  It entered competitive ''Dota 2'' in November 2023.

==History==
Team Falcons, formerly '''Falcons Esports''', is a multidisciplinary esports organization based in Saudi Arabia.  It was founded by [[easportsfc:Msdossary|Msdossary]] in 2017, and has since expanded to multiple esports titles.  In 2020, the organization fielded ''Dota 2'' rosters in Saudi tournaments such as [[Saudi_eLeagues/2020|Saudi eLeagues 2020]], but the organization had larger ambitions.  Believed to be funded by the Saudi Royal Family,<ref>{{cite web|last=Taifalos|first=Nicholas|url=https://dotesports.com/apex-legends/news/apex-legends-imperialhal-genburten-and-zer0-join-forces-on-saudi-backed-superteam|title= Apex legends ImperialHal, Genburten, and Zer0 join forces on Saudi-backed superteam|work=Dot Esports|date=28 May 2024|accessdate=1 June 2024}}</ref> it entered ''Dota 2'' once again in November 2023 with the signing of [[skiter]], [[Malr1ne]], [[ATF]], [[Cr1t-]], [[Sneyking]] and manager [[AfrOmoush]].  Team Falcons repeated this approach &mdash; signing star players to mould into a team &mdash; in multiple esports titles between 2022 and 2024.

With the ending of the [[Dota Pro Circuit]] in 2023, the [[ESL Pro Tour]] added a {{abbr|MENA|Middle East and North Africa}} region for its upcoming season, with the first challenge for Team Falcons being the [[ESL_One/Kuala_Lumpur/2023/MENA/Closed_Qualifier|ESL One Kuala Lumpur 2023: MENA Closed Qualifier]], after receiving a bye in the [[ESL_One/Kuala_Lumpur/2023/MENA/Open_Qualifier/1|Open Qualifier]] due to a lack of teams.  Despite the roster's relative lack of time to gel, the team got off to a solid start with a 3-1 win over [[PSG Quest]] in the Grand Final.  The team further proved it was no fluke with a win in [[Pinnacle_25_Year_Anniversary_Show|Pinnacle: 25 Year Anniversary Show]], despite using multiple standins, showcasing its core strength as a team.

At [[ESL_One/Kuala_Lumpur/2023|ESL One Kuala Lumpur 2023]], the team initially showcased its strength, going 7-3 in the Group Stages, but failed to win a series in the Playoffs, finishing 5th-6th &mdash; still an admirable result for the fledgling team.  The turn of the year, however, firmly put the team as the strongest in the world. [[Aui_2000]] joined as coach, in time for three straight qualifiers for [[BetBoom_Dacha/Dubai/2024/MENA/Closed_Qualifier|BetBoom Dacha Dubai 2024]], [[DreamLeague/Season_22/MENA/Closed_Qualifier|DreamLeague Season 22]] and [[ESL_One/Birmingham/2024/MENA/Closed_Qualifier|ESL One Birmingham 2024]] saw them stamp their dominance with first-place in all three tournaments, with all Grand Finals finishing 3-0.  A second place at [[Elite League/1|Elite League Season 1]] and third at [[PGL/Wallachia/1|PGL Wallachia Season 1]] were relative blips for Team Falcons, with the team returning to form with a commanding 3-0 Grand Finals win over [[Gaimin Gladiators]] in [[DreamLeague/Season_23|DreamLeague Season 23]].  Their results earned them the top spot in the [[ESL Pro Tour/Leaderboard/Season 2|ESL Pro Tour]], granting them a direct invite both to [[Riyadh Masters/2024|Riyadh Masters 2024]] and [[The International/2024|The International 2024]], as one of the favourites.

